{
  "dom_id": "step-q95108",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $p(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂p}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
  "worked_solution": "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $p(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂p}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂p}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂p}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂p}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $n$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂p}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂x} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂p}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
